[Black "Chapter Summary: Key Takeaways"]
[Result "*"]

 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Defining space: Space is a tricky concept.. You can think of space as meaning the control of a higher number of squares than your opponent, which generally but not always provides a positional advantage. (2) The space advantage:. If the extra squares under your control have a restrictive effect on your opponent’s army, then you are likely to have anything from a slight to a winning advantage. (3) The link between piece play and structure: The same structure can lead to radically different evaluations, depending on the way in which each side’s pieces are arranged. Space cannot be considered independently, it must be assessed in relation to the piece configuration. (4) The dark side of space: Space is not without its problems. An advanced pawn formation can leave weaknesses behind, which the opponent can exploit by a variety of methods. (5) Techniques to combat space: Undermining (via for example pawn breaks or sacrifices) is one popular method with its goal being to remove the space advantage. Overextension is a second powerful technique, looking to target any weaknesses created through grabbing space too quickly. (6) Problems of combating space: Fighting against space is not without its problems. If you mistime a pawn break, for example, then you may allow your opponent to transform the structure in a way that favors them. (7) Winning with the space advantage: If unchallenged, a space advantage can grow until a combination of advanced outposts, piece restriction, or pawn breaks can cause a collapse. It’s important to avoid allowing your opponent to have a free hand when pursuing a space-grabbing strategy since otherwise you are likely to get steamrolled. (8) Space & the centre: Space inevitably overlaps with central control. This is because superior central control is usually accompanied by a space advantage. The next chapter, on central control, will help to reinforce some of the topics covered in this one. } 1. { -KEY- } -- 
 *

