 ( { Should White play } 1. d5 { here, or } 1. dxe5 $2 ) 
1. { -KEY- } d5 $1 { Correct! As a general rule, the advance will tend to be good if two conditions hold true: a) Black cannot jump the knight to the ideal central square of d4. b) There is a lot of material on the board (making more space a more valuable asset). In this case, both of these hold true since all the pieces remain on the board for both sides. Meanwhile, ...Nd4 is not an option for Black since White has too much control over that square. } ( 1. dxe5 $2 { This move would give away most of White's advantage. For example: } Nxe5 2. Nxe5 Qxd1 3. Rxd1 Bxe5 $11 { The extra space is not significant, since the reduction in material on the board tends to reduce the advantageous nature of more space or central control. } ) 
Ne7 { Here White can continue in several ways. The simple O-O is strong, while the a2-a4 push is quite thematic, looking to combine the space grab in the centre with a queenside space grab. Concretely, this approach leaves Black with very limited options for their knight on b6. For example: } ( 1... Nd4 $2 2. Nxd4 exd4 3. Bxd4 $18 { White is a clean pawn to the good. } ) 
2. a4 $16 { White now expands on the queenside, and in the future they may wish to play h2-h4-h5 collecting space across all parts of the board. } 
