 ( { Choose between } 0... Nxe4 0. f5 { (grabbing space) or } 1. fxe5 { (opening up the f-file). } ) 
1. { -KEY- } fxe5 $1 { Correct! Oftentimes it can be very hard to judge which decision is right, but a key question to keep in mind is whether you can keep the space advantage stable or whether it can be undermined as in this example. } ( 1. f5 $2 { This natural move would typically be very strong, locking down the kingside and - in the long run - landing Black in serious trouble. The problem with this strategy is that here the pawns are overextended and Black can immediately take undermining actions: } d5 $1 2. exd5 Nxd5 $11 { The position is around equal now, since White's central chain has been fractured. } ) 
dxe5 2. b4 $16 { White is in command, with pressure along the semi open f-file and a space advantage on the queenside. } 
