 { Black is in trouble here, with White advancing dangerously on the queenside. In such situations, you will often need to find tactical opportunities in order to survive. Black to play and find the only idea that allows them to avoid a losing position. } 1... { -KEY- } Bxc5 $1 { Black initiates a temporary piece sacrifice. } ( 1... Qc8 $2 { A typical passive move like this would result in a fast collapse of Black's position. For example: } 2. bxc6 bxc6 3. Qb1 { With the queen off the d3 square, the tactical ideas with ...Bxc5 no longer work. } Be7 4. Ra7 Bd8 { Covering the c7 square. } 5. Na4 Ne4 6. Nb6 Bxb6 7. cxb6 { White has transformed their space advantage into a strong passed pawn on b6, which will soon cost Black decisive material. Black's position is already resignable. } ) 
2. dxc5 e5 $1 { Correct! White has nothing better than to take the pawn on e5 since a move like Bf4-g3 will be met by ...e4, winning back the piece owing to the fork. } 3. Nxe5 Nxe5 4. Qd4 Ned7 5. Bd6 $14 { White still stands a bit better owing to the extra space and the rather strong d6 bishop. However, with some clever maneuvering such as ...Nd7-f8-e6, Black has good chances to maintain the balance. A key point is that the tactical sequence led to the exchange of some material, easing the constrictive effect of White's space advantage. } 
